title: The boy’s face was the last you saw. His eyes. God help him, his eyes
new-page: true

Luckau, 1938.

It has been two years since you were arrested, sentenced to high treason. Eight years in a Zuchthaus by the Fascist state. 

You are Erdman, you are twenty four years old. You became a fugitive at eighteen, with the take over of the NSDAP over Germany. Though, you had already seen men die at the hands of the police.

You had been convited of high treason. They knew what you were doing, you were organising the apparatus of the resistance towards the fascist rule just as you were as a stress fighter against the Republicans.

The prison was old. Late 19th century, no doubt, cellular. Brick, wings of stacked cells, there was an iron galleries. Thisg rinding, cold, hungry place, not a camp though, like you had heard of. But they worked you nonetheless.

Count, work, count, lockup. You weaved, sometimes, sometimes you sorted, sometimes it was brush making. Twelve, thirteen, fourteen hours a day, for rotten food you'd not feed to a animal.

You glanced at your comrades, the only way to speak, if you talk you'll be beaten. Sometimes you wish for a beating than to take more from the black bread. You got no parcels, your family, what happened to them? You weren't sure.

The Wachtmeister were old men, old men with contempt for you and you contempt for them. Loyal lackeys of the old order now hounding for a new order which was the same but dressed in Fascist gaberdine over Royal Regalia. 

But even here, in this dark prsion cell, hope lives on. The KPD persists, the revolution persists in these cells, not for the rats, the Spitzel, nor the beatings of the guards, nothing can kill hope because it dies last, even when the body has given up.

You pray, though you aren't religious, keep the heart straight even as body falters in this cause. This is a sufferance that you endure for the hope of a better tomorrow, an end to this brown plague upon the whole world. 

You have not came this far, the blood spilled by the police, by the Army or by these Fascists, you already lost Karl Libeknecht, and Rosa Luxemburg fell by the hands of a murderer, that old man back in May, in Berlin, fell by a bullet. Your comrades, they vanished.

The weird, the crippled, the sick and the dying, those who fought against a world that churned them out, but you will keep fighting for them. You were taught by a friend, a Lagerleitung man, a veteran of the old days.
  
Dem Karl Liebknecht dem haben wir's geschooren, Der Rosa Luxemburg reichen wir die hand, 
